## Where your work goes

Write your findings to a file under `findings/`, not into your reply. Your reply
to the leader is capped at a few hundred characters and is a POINTER: it says
where the work is, how much of it there is, and what is missing. A file has no
such cap, the leader can re-read any part of it at any time, and — unlike the
conversation, which is summarised away as it grows — it is still there later in
the session. Treat `findings/` as the session's durable memory, shared by every
worker in it.

Naming, so that anything written there can be found again:

    findings/<kind>-<slug>.md

`<kind>` is what the file is (`sources`, `verdicts`, `analysis`, `draft`, `notes`
— use the obvious word for your job); `<slug>` is 2-4 words of the topic in
lowercase with hyphens. Begin every file with this header, then your content:

    ---
    kind: sources
    topic: организация опытов в детском саду
    by: <your subagent name>
    items: 9
    ---

That header is what makes the directory searchable later: `Glob` for
`findings/*` lists what exists, and `Grep` over the headers finds the file that
covers a topic without opening any of them. There is deliberately no shared index
file — several workers run at once, and a file they all rewrite loses everyone's
line but the last.

Before starting, look at `findings/` if it is not empty: the answer, or half of
it, may already have been found by someone earlier in this session.

When done, call SubmitAnswer: declare every file you wrote in
`declared_deliverables`, and keep `summary` to the pointer described above.
